                <p>\( \let\bibref\eqref&nbsp;%make-command_bibref-equivalent-to-eqref\)Frenkel excitons are responsible for electronic energy transport in organic chromophore aggregates, photosynthetic light harvesting complexes, and organic photovoltaic devices. The transport is characterized either as a diffusive process of exciton hopping via transition dipole interactions or, if electronic interactions are strong enough, as coherent wavepackets. In plants, algae and photosynthetic bacteria, light harvesting takes place within complexes of numerous spatially organized and energetically matched chromophores such as bacterochlorophylls or phycobilins. Exciton transport is used to describe the how the excitation energy from light absorption is funneled through these chromophores to a reaction center where charge separation occurs. In molecular aggregates and organic semiconductors, Frenkel excitons are used to describe the optical absorption spectrum, energy transport, and light emission from these materials. These properties play an important role in the design of organic solar cells and OLEDs.</p>

<p>To investigate Frenkel excitons, we will investigate a model for a linear aggregate of \(N\) identical molecules periodically arranged along \(x\) by a lattice spacing \(\alpha\). The length of the chain is \(L=\alpha N\). The molecules are indexed with integer values (\(n\)) between \(0\) and \(N-1\). (<a class="mt-self-link" title="20.2: Frenkel excitons in molecular aggregates" href="#Fig.+1+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" rel="internal">Figure 20.2.1</a>). Each molecule has a transition dipole moment \(\mu\), which makes an angle \(\beta\) with the axis of the chain (\(x\)). Each molecule is described by a two-level electronic system with a ground state \(|g\rangle\) and excited state \(|e\rangle\), and electronic excitation moves an electron from the ground state of one molecule to an unoccupied orbital of the same molecule.&nbsp;</p>

<figure><a id="Fig.+1+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" name="Fig.+1+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates"></a><img alt="Schematic illustration of a series of loops connected with vertical lines, symbolizing a flow or linkage in a system." class="internal default" loading="lazy" style="width: 399px; height: 174px;" width="399px" height="174px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/499925/Picture1.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=399&amp;height=174" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\): Illustration of the variables describing the linear chain of coupled chromophores equally spaced by \(\alpha\) along \(x\).</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>In the absence of interactions, we can specify the state of the system \(\left|\psi\right\rangle\) by identifying whether each molecule is in the excited or ground state. If the state of molecule \(n\) within the chain is \(\varphi _{n}\), which can take on values of \(g\) or \(e\), then the state of the system is given by the product state</p>

<p>\[\left| \psi&nbsp; \right\rangle&nbsp; = \left| \varphi _{0},\varphi _{1},\varphi _{2} \cdots \varphi _{n} \cdots \varphi _{N - 1} \right\rangle&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.01}\]</p>

<p>This representation is referred to as the <em>site basis</em>, since it is expressed in terms of the site of each molecule in the chain. For simplicity we write the ground state of the system as&nbsp;</p>

<p>\[\left| G \right\rangle&nbsp; = \left| g,g,g&nbsp; &nbsp;\ldots&nbsp; ,g \right\rangle&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.02}\]</p>

<p>If we excite one molecule within the aggregate, we have a singly excited (one-exciton) state in which the \(n^{th}\) molecule is excited, so that</p>

<p>\[\left| \psi&nbsp; \right\rangle&nbsp; = \left| g,g,g, \ldots ,e,&nbsp; &nbsp;\ldots&nbsp; ,g \right\rangle&nbsp; \equiv \left| n \right\rangle&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.03}\]</p>

<p>For shorthand, we identify this product state as \(|n\rangle\). For our model, the singly excited state is assigned a site energy \(\varepsilon _{0}\) corresponding to the electronic energy gap.&nbsp;</p>

<figure><a id="Fig.+2+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" name="Fig.+2+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates"></a><img alt="Diagram illustrating energy levels of an atom, showing excited states above the ground state." class="internal default" loading="lazy" style="width: 401px; height: 237px;" width="401px" height="237px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/499926/Picture2.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=401&amp;height=237" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{2}\): Energy level diagram for the \(N\) degenerate sites with excited state enegy \(\varepsilon_0\), and the resulting exciton band that is split due to coupling.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>In the absence of coupling, the singly excited states are \(N\)-fold degenerate, corresponding to a single excitation at any of the \(N\) sites. When coupling between molecules is introduced, the mixing of these degenerate states leads to exciton states (<a class="mt-self-link" title="20.2: Frenkel excitons in molecular aggregates" href="#Fig.+2+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" rel="internal">Figure 20.2.2</a>). The coupling between molecule \(n\) and molecule \(n\prime\) is given by the matrix element \(V_{nn'}\). We will assume that a molecule interacts only with its two nearest neighbors, and that each pairwise interaction has a magnitude \(J\):</p>

<p>\[V_{nn'} = J \delta _{n,n' \pm 1} \label{eq20.2.04}\]</p>

<p>We will also assume that \(V\) takes the form of a <a title="19.2: Resonance Energy Transfer and Transition Dipole Coupling" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/19%3A_Energy_and_Charge_Transfer/19.02%3A_Resonance_Energy_Transfer_and_Transition_Dipole_Coupling" rel="internal">transition dipole interaction</a>. The coupling strength \(J\) is given by \(J=\kappa \mu^2 / \alpha^3 \), where \(\mu\) is the electronic transition dipole moment, and \(\kappa\) is the orientational factor we described before. \(\kappa\) dictates that when the transition dipole angle \(\beta \lt&nbsp; 54.7&deg;\), the sign of the coupling is negative (\(J&nbsp; \lt&nbsp; 0\)). This arises from an offset stack of chromophores or head-to-tail arrangement, which is known as a \(J\)-aggregate (after Edwin Jelley). In contrast when \(\beta \gt&nbsp; 54.7&deg;\) then \(J&nbsp; \gt&nbsp; 0\), and the system is known as an \(H\)-aggregate (<a class="mt-self-link" title="20.2: Frenkel excitons in molecular aggregates" href="#Fig.+3+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" rel="internal">Figure 20.2.3</a>).</p>

<figure><a id="Fig.+3+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" name="Fig.+3+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates"></a><img alt="Illustration showing J and H aggregates with corresponding angles and J values, indicating their properties in molecular aggregates." class="internal default" loading="lazy" style="width: 399px; height: 230px;" width="399px" height="230px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/499929/Picture3.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=399&amp;height=230" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{3}\): \(J\)- and \(H\)-aggregates differ in the configuration of transition dipole moments in the aggregate, which determines the sign of the coupling.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>With these definitions, we can write the Frenkel exciton Hamiltonian for the aggregate in terms of a system Hamiltonian \(H_0\) that reflects the possibility of an electronic excitation at each site \(H_S\) and the couplings between sites \(V\)</p>

<p>\[\begin{array}{rl}<br />
&nbsp; H_{0} \kern-.8em &amp;= H_{S} + V \\<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp;&amp;= \varepsilon _{0}\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{N} \left| n \right\rangle \left\langle n \right|&nbsp; + J\sum\limits_{&nbsp; &nbsp;n',n = 0 }^{N - 1} \left| {n'} \right\rangle \left\langle n \right|&nbsp; \delta _{n,n' \pm 1}&nbsp;<br />
\end{array}&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.05}\]</p>

<p>The Hamiltonian takes the form of an \(N \times N\) tri-diagonal matrix, and the exciton states are the eigenstates of this Hamiltonian.&nbsp; Written in second quantization, this Hamiltonian is</p>

<p>\[H_{0} = \varepsilon _{0}\sum\limits_{n} a_n^{\dagger}a_{n}&nbsp; + J\sum\limits_{n \ne n'} a_{n'}^{\dagger}a_{n}&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.06}\]</p>

<p>where \(a_n^{\dagger}\) and \(a_{n}\) are the Pauli creation and annihilation operators for fermions.&nbsp;</p>

<p>To determine the exciton states, we look for solutions in the form of an expansion in the site basis wavefunctions:&nbsp;</p>

<p>\[\left| \psi (x) \right\rangle&nbsp; = \sum\limits_{n = 0}^{N - 1} c_{n}(x) \left| \varphi _{n}(x - x_{n}) \right\rangle&nbsp; &nbsp;\label{eq20.2.07}\]</p>

<p>This expression indicates that the wavefunction of an excited molecule at each site is the same; only its participation in an exciton wavefunction. Thus, the expansion coefficients \(c_n\) carry a position dependence along \(x\), and the molecular wavefunction for molecule \(n\) is centered at \(x_n=n\alpha\).&nbsp;</p>

<p>To determine the expansion coefficients, we will start by applying periodic boundary conditions, meaning that all molecules (including the ends of the chain) are coupled to their two nearest neighbors. This approximation is reasonable for long chains, and it effectively means we are describing the states of an \(N\)-molecule aggregate within an infinite chain. In terms of the Hamiltonian eq. (\ref{eq20.2.05}), this means that we allow \(n^\prime\) to take on the values \(-1\) to \(N\), or, in its matrix form, we also add elements that couple the first and last member of the chain: \(J_{0,N - 1} = J_{N - 1,0} = J\).&nbsp;</p>

<p>Rather than diagonalizing the Hamiltonian, we can take advantage of its translational symmetry to obtain the eigenstates. The symmetry of the Hamiltonian is such that it is unchanged by any integral number of translations along the chain. That is, the results are unchanged for any summation in eqs. (\ref{eq20.2.05}) and (\ref{eq20.2.07}) over \(N\) consecutive integers. Similarly, the molecular wavefunction at any site is unchanged by such a translation. Written in terms of a displacement operator \(D = e^{ip_{x}\alpha /\hbar }\) that shifts the molecular wavefunction by one lattice constant \(\alpha\), the eigenstates are</p>

<p>\[\left| \varphi \left( x + n\alpha&nbsp; \right) \right\rangle&nbsp; = D^{n} \left| \varphi \left( x \right) \right\rangle&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.08}\]</p>

<p>Thus, when the eigenstates are displaced by a lattice constant, they will vary only by a phase shift.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>

<p>\[\left| \psi (x + \alpha ) \right\rangle&nbsp; = e^{ik\alpha }\left| \psi (x) \right\rangle&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.09}\]</p>

<p><span id="cke_bm_125S" style="display:none;">&nbsp;</span><span id="cke_bm_124S" style="display:none;">&nbsp;</span>Here \(k\), the wavevector or reciprocal lattice vector, is a real quantity. Thus the expansion coefficients in eq. (\ref{eq20.2.07}) will only vary between sites by a spatially varying phase factor, and their amplitude will reflect an excitation spread equally among the \(N\) sites.&nbsp; This observation is known as Bloch&rsquo;s theorem, which states that the eigenstates of a periodic potential \(u(x)\) will be a product of a spatially varying phase factor and the periodic function, \(u\):</p>

<p>\[\left| \psi (x) \right\rangle&nbsp; = e^{ikx}u(x) \label{eq20.2.10}\]</p>

<p>Now let&rsquo;s investigate \(k\). If the linear chain has \(N\) molecules, the eigenstates must remain unchanged when undergoing a translation by the length of the chain \(L = \alpha&nbsp;N\):</p>

<p>\[\left| \psi (x_{n} + L) \right\rangle&nbsp; = \left| \psi (x_{n}) \right\rangle&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.11}\]</p>

<p>Therefore, we see that our wavefunctions must satisfy \(e^{ikL} = 1\), or\(\bibref{Cite1} %in-text-citation\)</p>

<p>\[Nk\alpha&nbsp; = 2\pi m \label{eq20.2.12}\]</p>

<p>where \(k\) is the wavenumber, and \(m\) is an index that labels the exciton eigenstates of an \(N\) molecule chain. Since there are \(N\) sites on the chain, unique solutions to eq. (\ref{eq20.2.12}) require that \(m\) can only take on \(N\) consecutive integer values, for a total of \(N\) exciton eigenstates. From eq. (\ref{eq20.2.12}), we can express the wavenumber as&nbsp;</p>

<p>\[k_{m} = \frac{2\pi }{\alpha }\frac{m}{N} \label{eq20.2.13}\]</p>

<p>We see that for an \(N\) site lattice, \(m\) takes on the \(N\) consecutive integer values such that \(k_m\) varies over a \(2\pi\) range of angles. Like the site index \(n\), there is no unique choice of \(m\). By convention, \(k_m\) is chosen such that</p>

<p>\[ - \frac{\pi }{\alpha }&nbsp; \lt&nbsp; &nbsp;k_{m}&nbsp; \leqslant \frac{\pi }{\alpha } \label{eq20.2.14}\]</p>

<p>Then the corresponding values of \(m\) are integers from \(-(N-1)/2\) to \((N-1)/2\) if there are an odd number of lattice sites or \(-(N-2)/2\) to \(N/2\) for an even number of sites. For example, a 20-molecule chain would have \(m = -9, -8, \ldots 9,10\).</p>

<p>These findings lead to the general form for the exciton eigenstates</p>

<p>\[\left| k_{m} \right\rangle&nbsp; = \frac{1}{\sqrt {N} }\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{N - 1} e^{ink_{m}\alpha } \left| n \right\rangle&nbsp; &nbsp;\label{eq20.2.15}\]</p>

<p>The factor of \(N^{-1/2}\) assures proper normalization of the wavefunction. Comparing eqs. (\ref{eq20.2.15}) and (\ref{eq20.2.07}), we see that the expansion coefficients for the \(n^{th}\) site of the \(m^{th}\) eigenstate is&nbsp;</p>

<p>\[c_{m,n} = \frac{1}{\sqrt {N} }e^{ink_{m}\alpha } = \frac{1}{\sqrt {N} }e^{2\pi i m n/N} \label{eq20.2.16}\]</p>

<p>These coefficients have fixed amplitude and vary only by phase, which can be quantified through:&nbsp;</p>

<p>\[\phi _{m,n} = \frac{2\pi&nbsp; m n }{N} \label{eq20.2.17}\]</p>

<p>Thus, the exciton eigenstates are plane-waves, and eq. (\ref{eq20.2.16}) gives the matrix elements for the transformation from the site basis to the exciton basis.&nbsp; From eq. (\ref{eq20.2.16}), we see that for state \(\left| k_{0} \right\rangle \), with \(m = 0\), the phase angle is the same for all sites. In other words, the transition dipoles of the chain will oscillate in-phase, adding constructively for all sites. For the case that \(k_m = \pi/\alpha\), each site is out-of-phase with its nearest neighbors.&nbsp; In <a class="mt-self-link" title="20.2: Frenkel excitons in molecular aggregates" href="#Fig.+4+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" rel="internal">Figure 20.2.4</a>, we show an example of how the expansion coefficients and phase of the site wavefunctions vary as a function of exciton state for a 12-molecule aggregate.</p>

<figure><a id="Fig.+4+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" name="Fig.+4+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates"></a><img alt="Graph showing multiple colored lines representing different data sets converging towards a horizontal red line in the lower part." class="internal default" loading="lazy" style="width: 501px; height: 334px;" width="501px" height="334px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/499927/Picture4.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=501&amp;height=334" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{4}\): &nbsp;Phase \(\phi_{m,n}\) for the \(m=0, 1, 3\) and \(6\) exciton states of an \(N=12\) molecule aggregate. For \(m=0\) all sites are in phase, whereas for \(m= N / 2 = 6\), all sites are out of phase with their neighbors</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Finally, let&rsquo;s solve for the exciton energy eigenvalues by calculating the expectation value of the Hamiltonian operator, eq. (\ref{eq20.2.05})</p>

<p>\[E(k_{m}) = \left\langle m \right|H_{0}\left| m \right\rangle&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.18}\]</p>

<p>From the two terms in \(H_0\), we calculate two contributions to the energy:</p>

<p>\[\left\langle m \right|H_{S}\left| m \right\rangle&nbsp; = \frac{1}{N}\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{N - 1} \varepsilon _{0}&nbsp; = \varepsilon _{0} \label{eq20.2.19}\]\[\begin{align}\begin{aligned}<br />
\left\langle m \right|V\left| m \right\rangle&nbsp; &amp;&nbsp; = \frac{1}{N}\sum\limits_{n = 0}^{N - 1} \left\{ e^{ik_{m}\alpha }\left\langle n - 1 \right|V\left| n \right\rangle&nbsp; + e^{ - ik_{m}\alpha }\left\langle n + 1 \right|V\left| n \right\rangle&nbsp; \right\}&nbsp; \\<br />
&nbsp; &amp;&nbsp; = 2J\cos (k_{m}\alpha ) \\<br />
\end{aligned}\end{align}&nbsp; \label{eq20.2.20}\]</p>

<p>So that the total energy of the \(m^{th}\) exciton state is</p>

<p>\[E(k_{m}) = \varepsilon _{0} + 2J\cos (k_{m}\alpha ) \label{eq20.2.21}\]</p>

<p>We predict that the band of exciton states varies in energy between \(\varepsilon _{0} - 2J\) and \(\varepsilon _{0} + 2J\). If we take \(J\) as negative, as is expected for the case of \(J\)-aggregates, then \(k = 0\) is at the bottom of the band, as illustrated in <a class="mt-self-link" title="20.2: Frenkel excitons in molecular aggregates" href="#Fig.+5+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" rel="internal">Figure 20.2.5</a> for an \(N=12\) aggregate. For this even-numbered case, there are single states at the band extrema, \(m=0\) at the bottom of the band and \(m=6\) at the top, and pairs of degenerate \(\pm m\) states in between.&nbsp; The density of states&mdash;the number of states per energy interval&mdash;is greatest at the band extrema and least in the center of the band. For the case of \(H\)-aggregates, in which the coupling is positive&nbsp; (\(J \gt 0)\), the dispersion relationship and energy ordering is inverted, with the \(m=0\) \(\left(k=0\right)\) state at the top of the band and \(m=6\) \(\left(k=\pi/a\right)\) at the bottom.</p>

<figure><a id="Fig.+5+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates" name="Fig.+5+Frenkel+excitons+in+molecular+aggregates"></a><img alt="Graph showing a downward-opening curve with red data points, labeled axes, and a legend on the right." class="internal default" loading="lazy" style="width: 599px; height: 327px;" width="599px" height="327px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/499928/Picture5.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=599&amp;height=327" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{5}\): Dispersion relation (left) and corresponding energy level structure (right) for a \(J\)-aggregate (\(J \lt 0\)) with \(N=12\). The points indicate the energy and wavevector of the 12 exciton states.&nbsp; The dashed line represents the dispersion relationship in the limit \(N\rightarrow\infty\).</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>One assumption of our model is that only the Coulombic coupling between nearest neighbors is taken into account. If longer-range couplings between sites are also included, many of the qualitative observations remain unchanged; however, the exciton energies are modified. Including long-range interactions has the effect of shifting the exciton band asymmetrically about \(\varepsilon _{0}\), such that the bottom of the band is at \(E(k_{0}) = \varepsilon_{0} + 2.4J\) with \(J \lt 0\), and the top of the band is at \(E(k_{N}) = \varepsilon_{0} - 1.8J\).</p>

<p>The finite size of typical molecular aggregates means that periodic boundary conditions may not be appropriate to our calculations, and in fact removing this condition has effects on the exciton states and energies. Under these open boundary conditions, the molecules at the end of the chain are only coupled to their one nearest neighbor. In this case, it is helpful to label the sites from \(n = 1,2 \ldots N\), and the exciton states \(m = 1,2 \ldots N\). Under those conditions, one can solve for the eigenstates using use the boundary condition that \(\psi&nbsp; = 0\) at sites \(0\) and \(N+1\). The change in boundary conditions gives <em>sine </em>solutions for the exciton wavefunctions</p>

<p>\[\left| k_{m} \right\rangle&nbsp; = \sqrt {\frac{2}{N + 1}}&nbsp; \sum\limits_{n = 1}^{N} \sin \left( \frac{\pi mn}{N + 1} \right) \left| n \right\rangle&nbsp; &nbsp;\label{eq20.2.22}\]</p>

<p>and the exciton energies are</p>

<p>\[E_{m} = \varepsilon _{0} + 2J\cos \left( \frac{\pi m}{N + 1} \right)&nbsp; \qquad&nbsp; m = 1,2 \ldots N \label{eq20.2.23}\]</p>

<p>These expressions can also be written in terms of the wavenumber \(k_m=\pi m/\alpha\left(N+1\right)\). Note that the exciton states in eq. (\ref{eq20.2.22}) take on the form of particle-in-a-box eigenstates, where the \(m^{th}\) state has \((m-1)\) nodes. Equation (\ref{eq20.2.22}) also expresses a transformation from the site basis to the exciton (eigen) basis where the transformation matrix elements are</p>

<p>\[T_{mn} = \sqrt {\frac{2}{N + 1}} \sin \left( \frac{\pi mn}{N + 1} \right) \label{eq20.2.24}\]</p>

<p>Extending the Frenkel exciton model to properly account for three-dimensions can rapidly become complex depending on how anisotropic the system is in terms of structure and interactions, and the types of intermolecular couplings that need to be accounted for. We can take one step in this direction by considering a simple cubic lattice of molecules, where the lattice constant \(\alpha\), nearest neighbor couplings \(J\), site energy \(\varepsilon_0\), and chain length \(L=N\alpha\) are all uniform in \(x,y,\) and \(z\), and no couplings exist between the three dimensions. We still only consider the one-exciton states in which a single excitation exists across the whole lattice, so we need to index the location of that excitation site with \(|n_x,n_y,n_z\rangle\). The exciton states take on the same form</p>

<p>\[\left|\mathbf{k}_{\mathbf{m}}\right\rangle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N^3}} \sum_{n_x, n_y, n_z} e^{i\left(k_x n_x \alpha+k_y n_y \alpha+k_z n_z \alpha\right)}\left|n_x, n_y, n_z\right\rangle \label{eq20.2.25}\]</p>

<p>where the summation is taken over \(n_j\), \(j=\left\{x,y,z\right\}\), and \(n_j\) runs from 0 to \(N-1\) in each axis. Here the exciton state \(\left|\mathbf{k}_\mathbf{m}\right\rangle\) is defined through three independent wavenumbers \(k_j\) with \(j=\left\{x,y,z\right\}\), which are indexed through \(m_j\):&nbsp;</p>

<p>\[k_{j} = \frac{2\pi m_{j}}{N\alpha } \qquad&nbsp; j = \left\{ x,y,z \right\} \label{eq20.2.26}\]</p>

<p>For open boundary conditions, the eigenstates are written as products of sines instead, just as&nbsp;</p>

<p>expected for the 3D particle in a box.&nbsp; The corresponding energies are&nbsp;</p>

<p>\[E\left( \mathbf{k}_\mathbf{m} \right) = \varepsilon _{0} + 2J\left[ \cos \left( k_{x}\alpha&nbsp; \right) + \cos\left( k_{y}\alpha&nbsp; \right) + \cos\left( k_{z}\alpha&nbsp; \right) \right] \label{eq20.2.27}\]</p>

<p>Here we see that the overall bandwidth for a large \(N\) is \(12J\), varying from \(\varepsilon_0+6J\) and \(\varepsilon_0-6J\), reflecting the value of \(k_j\) at the band extrema, and the bandstructure is a 3D surface rather than a line.&nbsp;</p>

